    "result_text": "The build is green. The full test suite (the single `AcceptanceTest`) passes with all changes in place.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented `namesakeCount` on owner creation \u2014 the number of existing owners (before this create) that share the same `firstName` and `lastName`, compared case-insensitively. Following the existing pattern used for the derived `customerCode`/`householdId` fields, it's computed at create time, persisted on the owner, and returned on both create and `GET /api/owners/{id}`.\n\nChanges:\n- **`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** \u2014 added `countNamesakes(firstName, lastName)` (streams `findAllOwners()`, matching trimmed + lower-cased names) plus a `normalizeName` helper, and set the value on the owner before saving (excludes the owner being created, since it counts before persist).\n- **`model/Owner.java`** \u2014 new `namesakeCount` field (`@Column(name = \"namesake_count\")`) with getter/setter.\n- **`mapper/OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 ignore `namesakeCount` in `toOwner(OwnerFieldsDto)` (it's a derived output, not a client input), consistent with `customerCode`/`householdId`.\n- **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added the read-only `namesakeCount` integer property to the `Owner` schema so the generated `OwnerDto` exposes it.\n- **`db/{hsqldb,h2,mysql,postgres}/schema.sql`** \u2014 added the `namesake_count` column (tests use hsqldb with `ddl-auto=none`, so the column must exist in the schema).\n\nThe new `AcceptanceTest.coreCountsNamesakes` and the full suite pass.",
